8617530</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wang J., Stuckey J. A., Wishart M. J., Dixon J. E. (2002) A unique carbohydrate binding domain targets the lafora disease phosphatase to glycogen. J. Biol. Chem. 277, 2377&#x2013;2380</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">11739371</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>DePaoli-Roach A. A., Contreras C. J., Segvich D. M., Heiss C., Ishihara M., Azadi P., Roach P. J. (2014) Glycogen phosphomonoester distribution in mouse models of the progressive myoclonic epilepsy, Lafora disease. J. Biol. Chem. 290, 841&#x2013;850</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC4294506</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">25416783</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>
Gayarre J., Duran-Tr&#xed;o L., Criado Garcia O., Aguado C., Juana-L&#xf3;pez L., Crespo I., Knecht E., Bovolenta P., Rodr&#xed;guez de C&#xf3;rdoba S. (2014) The phosphatase activity of laforin is dispensable to rescue Epm2a<sup>&#x2212;/&#x2212;</sup> mice from Lafora disease. Brain
137, 806&#x2013;818</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">24430976</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">38397161</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2024</Year><Month>02</Month><Day>26</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2025</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>30</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2073-4425</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>15</Volume><Issue>2</Issue><PubDate><Year>2024</Year><Month>Jan</Month><Day>27</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Genes</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Genes (Basel)</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Progressive Myoclonus Epilepsy: A Scoping Review of Diagnostic, Phenotypic and Therapeutic Advances.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">171</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3390/genes15020171</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>The progressive myoclonus epilepsies (PME) are a diverse group of disorders that feature both myoclonus and seizures that worsen gradually over a variable timeframe. While each of the disorders is individually rare, they collectively make up a non-trivial portion of the complex epilepsy and myoclonus cases that are seen in tertiary care centers. The last decade has seen substantial progress in our understanding of the pathophysiology, diagnosis, prognosis, and, in select disorders, therapies of these diseases. In this scoping review, we examine English language publications from the past decade that address diagnostic, phenotypic, and therapeutic advances in all PMEs. We then highlight the major lessons that have been learned and point out avenues for future investigation that seem promising.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zimmern</LastName><ForeName>Vincent</ForeName><Initials>V</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-5249-9427</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Division of Child Neurology, University of Texas Southwestern, Dallas, TX 75390, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Minassian</LastName><ForeName>Berge</ForeName><Initials>B</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Division of Child Neurology, University of Texas Southwestern, Dallas, TX 75390, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D000098583">Scoping Review</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2024</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>27</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>Switzerland</Country><MedlineTA>Genes (Basel)</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>101551097</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>2073-4425</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D006801" MajorTopicYN="N">Humans</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D009207" MajorTopicYN="Y">Myoclonus</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D020191" MajorTopicYN="Y">Myoclonic Epilepsies, Progressive</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000175" MajorTopicYN="N">diagnosis</QualifierName><QualifierName UI="Q000235" MajorTopicYN="N">genetics</QualifierName><QualifierName UI="Q000628" MajorTopicYN="N">therapy</QualifierName></MeshHeading></MeshHeadingList><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Lafora disease</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">North Sea PME</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Unverricht-Lundborg disease</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">acid ceramidase deficiency</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">myoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibers</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">progressive myoclonic epilepsy<